A||||9||wardog85GT||I've got serious brake problems...please help||||4||
Z||000000||wardog85GT||08-30-2001||10:50 PM||||My problem is that I 've recently replaced the two rear calipers and I get a real spongy pedal that goes to the floor. I adjusted my e-brake and it seems to be working fine.I've tried two rebuilt master cylinders from Advance and still have the problem. I acheive good pedal after a couple of pumps so that I can bleed them, but after I crank the car and staart rolling the pedal goes to the floor. Any comments are appreciated.||||reg||4
Z||000001||PBJ||08-30-2001||10:56 PM||||If you can pump the pedal up, indicates air in the brake lines. I have heard that it can be a long process to remove all the air, especially from the rear brake calipers.  I do beleive that you can read the ogre's process to bleed-I guess it works better than the manual (GM) way.  CLICK on the link to the ogre's cave.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||85gted||08-30-2001||10:59 PM||||Did you try gravity bleed all 4/wheels with the master cyl cap off for about 15 min keeping the master cyl. full then pump bleed them with slow pumps ,, sound like you still have air traped somewhere or have a leak . 85gted <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by wardog85GT:<BR><B>My problem is that I 've recently replaced the two rear calipers and I get a real spongy pedal that goes to the floor. I adjusted my e-brake and it seems to be working fine.I've tried two rebuilt master cylinders from Advance and still have the problem. I acheive good pedal after a couple of pumps so that I can bleed them, but after I crank the car and staart rolling the pedal goes to the floor. Any comments are appreciated.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||08-30-2001||11:05 PM||||You need to bleed them in order from longest line to shortest.<BR>So,<BR>Passenger rear first<BR>Driver rear second<BR>Passenger front third <BR>Driver front last. <P>Even changing the rears out you still could have air in the fronts. The gravity method works great for the initial bleed than within 3-4 bleeds all the air is out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||wardog85GT||08-30-2001||11:15 PM||||There is something else. When I bled the front brakes it drained fluid from the rear- most reservoir in the Mast. Cyl..||||reg||1
Z||000005||Primaris||08-31-2001||02:37 AM||||It drained fluid from the rear of the master cylinder when it was below the split between the two reservoirs?<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000006||wardog85GT||08-31-2001||05:50 PM||||I think so because the fluid in the rear res. was alot lower than the front res.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Primaris||08-31-2001||06:17 PM||||Well I think you may need to repalce your master cyl. then.  If you do don't forget to bench bleed it before installing it on the car.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-31-2001||07:33 PM||||Spongy pedal is nearly always air in the lines. It can be a bad MC. "Pedal Bleeding" a system can ruin the MC seals if there is trash built up in the MC. You replaced the MC so that leaves air.<P>Take the calipers off the bracket and turn them so the piston faces down. tap them with a block of wood or a PLASTIC or RUBBER mallot/hammer.<P>The rear pistons can trap air that is dificult to get out. If you want to pump the fluid thru them in that position, OK but put a block of wood in there to prevent caughing out the piston.<P>If the MC was replaced.... The MC MUST be bench bleed according to the instructions that should come with new one.<P>Unless there is a huge price diference or you just can't get new, don't buy rebuilt master cylinders.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||wardog85GT||09-01-2001||12:34 AM||||Thanx for the advice. I just bought an '84 Fiero parts car for $100 so I'll try the MC off of that one.||||reg||1
